# doc/source_pdfs — CACHED SOURCE DOCUMENTS that LIVE extractors read as their input
# (2026-08-01). The blanket *.pdf / *.xlsx rules above were silently swallowing these,
# even though three extractors' own headers describe them as "cached source, git-tracked":
#   derelict_sites_levy_extract.py:15   -> 2024_Derelict_Sites_Statistics.xlsx
#   noac_scorecard_extract.py:37        -> NOAC_LA_PerfInd_2024.pdf
#   noac_indicators_long_extract.py:11  -> NOAC_LA_PerfInd_2024.pdf
#   local_authority_arv_extract.py:42   -> 2026_Annual_Rates_on_Valuation.xlsx
# They are upstream-published one-off documents (DHLGH/NOAC), NOT regenerable by the
# pipeline and NOT mirrored to R2 (backup_to_r2.ps1 only covers bronze/silver/raw_bq), so
# an un-tracked copy existed on exactly one disk. Same survive-machine-failure rationale as
# the silver AFS facts below. ~5.8 MB total.
!doc/source_pdfs/2024_Derelict_Sites_Statistics.xlsx
!doc/source_pdfs/NOAC_LA_PerfInd_2024.pdf
!doc/source_pdfs/2026_Annual_Rates_on_Valuation.xlsx

# Raw server-download artifacts (LA procurement portals hand back .aspx/.ashx blobs that
# are really PDFs/HTML); these are regenerable downloads, never source — the *.pdf rule
# above misses them. One .aspx leaked into data/bronze/pdfs/ before this rule existed.
*.aspx
*.ashx
# Ad-hoc dated backup copies of data files (e.g. foo.parquet.bak_20260613) — manual
# safety copies taken before a risky rewrite; regenerable, never source. Two leaked into
# data/silver/parquet/ before this rule existed. Covers .bak and .bak_<date> suffixes.
*.bak
*.bak_*
